course: dictionary
lesson: 1889
title: Collision: crashing and clashing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A collision happens when things crash into each other while they are moving. The verb is collide: two cars collide, and the crash between them is a collision. Look at the clues around the word: «Trampoline parks have one person per bed and marshals to stop collisions.» The rules are there to stop people crashing into each other, so a collision must be a crash.
explain: The word can also mean a strong clash between ideas or people. «A collision of interests» means two people want different things and they clash. Check by swapping in your meaning: "a crash of interests" sounds odd, but "a clash of interests" fits the sentence, so that is the meaning here.
explain: In the corridor sentence, running causes collisions and falls. Those collisions are the moments when people bump and crash while they move, so you can see the word is about a sudden hit, not a quiet meeting.
fact: A collision is when two or more things crash into each other while moving.
fact: «A head-on collision» is when two things hit each other front to front.
fact: «In collision with» means crashing into something, like a bike in collision with a wall.
fact: «A collision course» means heading straight for a crash, or for a clash.
fact: In a game, collision detection checks whether two things touch, so a player is pushed back and cannot walk through a wall.
exercise: «Corridors are crowded and running causes collisions and falls.» What is a collision here? | A quiet walk along the corridor | A loud shout for help | A kind of door | *A crash between people moving
exercise: Which word is the verb that matches the noun collision? | collar | *collide | collect | collage
exercise: «They had a collision of interests about who should use the bike.» What does collision mean here? | A plan to share the bike | A friendly chat about bikes | A crash between two bikes | *A clash between two people who want different things
test: Two cars hit each other at a junction. What is this? | A sleepover | A garden party | A parade | *A collision
test: Which phrase means heading straight for a crash or a clash? | A quiet corner | A short break | A happy ending | *A collision course
exam: «Trampoline parks have one person per bed to stop collisions.» Which word from the sentence means crashes? | person | parks | bed | *collisions
